     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><span>On Tue, Mar 31, 2015 at 8:04 PM, Dean Attali <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href rel=\"nofollow\" data-email-masked>daat...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></span></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><span><div dir=\"ltr\">I noticed that tag attributes are stored as a named list in the `attribs` value of a shiny.tag<div><br></div><div>I wanted to add a class to a tag that already had a class, like so:</div><div><br></div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">shiny</span><span style=\"color:#660\">::</span><span style=\"color:#000\">tagAppendAttributes</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">shiny</span><span style=\"color:#660\">::</span><span style=\"color:#000\">div</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;hello&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">class</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;foo&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">class</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;bar&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div><br><div><br></div><div>This does work right and give me the desired output </div><div><br></div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#008\">&lt;div</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">class</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;foo bar&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#008\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">hello</span><span style=\"color:#008\">&lt;/div&gt;</span></div></code></div><br> <div><br></div><div>However, when I inspect the tag I see that the two classes are stored as two separate items</div><div><br></div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#606\">List</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> of </span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br> $ name </span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> chr </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;div&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br> $ attribs </span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#606\">List</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> of </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">..</span><span style=\"color:#000\">$ </span><span style=\"color:#008\">class</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> chr </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;foo&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">..</span><span style=\"color:#000\">$ </span><span style=\"color:#008\">class</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> chr </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;bar&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br> $ children</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#606\">List</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> of </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">..</span><span style=\"color:#000\">$ </span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> chr </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;hello&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> attr</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(*,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;class&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> chr </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;shiny.tag&quot;</span></div></code></div><div><br></div><div><br></div>This feels wrong, though it&#39;s probably not technically wrong? I was hoping the class would be a string &quot;foo bar&quot; but instead it just added &quot;bar&quot; as another class.  It does make it a bit more difficult to test for a specific class.<div><br></div><div>I&#39;m just wondering if there is a more correct way to do what I&#39;m doing (adding multiple values of the same attribute but not all at once) and if this behaviour is correct or undesired.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>Thanks ShinyRs!</div></div></span></blockquote></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
